From: =?utf-8?b?T3R0byBLZWvDpGzDpGluZW4=?= <otto@kekalainen.net>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 01:47:24 +0000
Subject: Document bashisms in scripts that are supposed to stay
 POSIX-compliant

Lintian reported bash-term-in-posix-shell for scripts that declare
/bin/sh as their interpreter:

    I: mariadb-client: bash-term-in-posix-shell '`echo "testing\c"' [usr/bin/mariadb-secure-installation:199]
    I: mariadb-server: bash-term-in-posix-shell '${BASH_VERSION' [usr/share/mariadb/wsrep_notify:86]
    I: mariadb-server: bash-term-in-posix-shell '[ "$url" ==' [usr/bin/mariadbd-safe:222]
    I: mariadb-server: bash-term-in-posix-shell 'let' [usr/bin/mariadbd-safe:41]
    I: mariadb-server: bash-term-in-posix-shell 'ulimit' [usr/bin/mariadbd-safe:718]
    I: mariadb-server: bash-term-in-posix-shell 'ulimit' [usr/bin/mariadbd-safe:838]
    I: mariadb-server-core: bash-term-in-posix-shell 'source tree,' [usr/bin/mariadb-install-db:100]

Only scripts/mysqld_safe.sh really uses non-POSIX shell features (local
variables and the == operator in test), but it should stay using sh
as mentioned in c10bed1.

The other reported scripts do run fine under /bin/sh and are only false
positives of the Lintian check:
- mariadb-secure-installation deliberately probes the shell's echo
  behaviour
- wsrep_notify intentionally checks for a Bash runtime and falls back
  to POSIX code when Bash is not available
- The `let` and `ulimit` hits in mariadbd-safe are respectively a word
  in a comment and a POSIX utility also provided by Dash
- `source tree,` is a word in a comment in mariadb-install-db

Also update the existing overrides to ensure paths and line numbers match.

Forwarded: https://github.com/MariaDB/server/pull/5866
---
 scripts/mysqld_safe.sh | 5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

diff --git a/scripts/mysqld_safe.sh b/scripts/mysqld_safe.sh
index f67dbfd..e32c8a2 100644
